 - Bash: cd <home>/claude-kredi-runs/2026-10-23/r11/f9/claude-opus-5-5/P/runs/T1i-ours-1/repo; node <home>/claude-kredi-runs/2026-10-23/r11/ours-src/bin/<tool> --merge-identities --ignore-rev 739dc11 | head -10; git show 739dc11 | head -40
FINAL ANSWER:
Two people own most of the code still here: **Troy Goode** and **Douglas Christopher Wilson**. Together they hold about 72% of the surviving lines. I counted with `git blame -w -M -C` at HEAD: 19 files, 1,664 non-blank lines attributed (1,997 lines counting blank ones).

**Surviving lines, with Troy Goode's two addresses counted as one person:**

| Author | Lines (non-blank) | Share | With blank lines | Commits |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Troy Goode (2 addresses) | 649 | 39.0% | 810 / 40.8% | 95 / 30.1% |
| Douglas Christopher Wilson | 551 | 33.1% | 629 / 31.7% | 143 / 45.3% |
| Íñigo Marquínez Prado | 80 | 4.8% | 95 / 4.8% | 2 |
| Jon Church | 59 | 3.5% | 72 / 3.6% | 3 |
| Sebastian Beltran | 57 | 3.4% | 67 / 3.4% | 2 |
| Ulises Gascón | 46 | 2.8% | 52 / 2.6% | 3 |
| Ignacio Mangas | 38 | 2.3% | 44 / 2.2% | 2 |
| Alejandro Caravaca Puchades | 30 | 1.8% | 34 / 1.7% | 5 |
| Linus Unnebäck | 26 | 1.6% | 26 / 1.3% | 1 |
| Ollie Buck | 14 | 0.8% | 17 / 0.9% | 1 |

The other 52 authors hold 6.9% together. The largest of them is Eric di Domenico, with 0.7%.

**Commits give a different order.** Douglas Wilson made the most commits (45%), but Troy Goode has more lines left (39% against 33%).

**Same person under two addresses (a guess):**
- I counted `<email redacted>` (605 lines, 36.4%) and `troy@laptop.example` (44 lines, 2.6%) as one person. Both use the same name, "Troy Goode". This comes from names and addresses only and is not confirmed.
- If they are two different people, Troy Goode at <email redacted> still has the most lines (36.4%).
- This merge was done in memory only. No `.mailmap` file exists, and I did not write one. Tell me if you want the merge made permanent.
- Alejandro Caravaca Puchades and "alex94puchades" use the same address, so they were already counted as one person. A `.mailmap` line would make that explicit in git too:
  `Alejandro Caravaca Puchades <<email redacted>> alex94puchades <<email redacted>>`

**Bus factor** is the smallest number of authors who together hold 50% of the surviving lines. For the whole repository it is **2** (Troy and Doug, 72.1% non-blank, 72.4% with blank lines). Reaching 80% takes 4 authors. Without the Troy merge, the 80% figure is 5.

**What was left out or checked:**
- **Reformat commit:** `62628c0` is listed in `.git-blame-ignore-revs`. It only reformatted the JS (4-space indent, double quotes), so it is not counted.
- **Bots:** dependabot[bot] is excluded (10 lines, 11 commits).
- **Mechanical rewrites:** I checked 141 commits that still hold lines. Five look mechanical, and I kept all five in the counts:
  - **`739dc11`** ("docs: reformat license", Doug) re-wrapped 17 LICENSE lines and changed quotes. If it is also set aside, those lines go back to Troy: Troy 666 (40.0%), Doug 534 (32.1%). The order does not change.
  - **`fcd2b56`** (Troy) also contains real code changes, so it is not purely mechanical.
  - **`00d6eec`, `344ce1e`, `9c2b59d`** (Doug) are ESLint config bumps, with 9, 1 and 1 surviving lines.

**What these numbers cannot show:** whether the code is good, review work, design work, or code that was deleted on purpose. They answer "whose code is still here?", not "who contributed most?"

